course: world
lesson: 186
title: "How do you know?"
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The best question in the world has four words: "How do you know?" It isn't rude; it asks for the road from the claim back to the evidence.
explain: Good answers point to something you can check: someone saw it, measured it, tested it fairly. Weak answers are "I just know", "everyone says it", "it was on a poster" or "it's an old saying".
explain: Toby says sharks can't get cancer. "How do you know?" "I just know." Ellie: "You 'just know'? Then where'd you hear it?" Scientists have found tumours in sharks, so the claim falls over.
explain: Many famous "facts" are myths that nobody checked: carrots for night vision, the 10% brain, gum staying seven years, ostriches burying their heads.
explain: Asking makes you a good thinker, not a pest, as long as you ask about the claim and not about whether the person is clever.
fact: "I just know" is not a reason; asking "How do you know?" is the quickest way to test a claim.
fact: Sharks can get cancer: scientists have found tumours (lumps of cancer) in sharks.
fact: Astronauts say the Great Wall of China is very hard or impossible to see from space with bare eyes, because it is long but narrow.
fact: One trick, like sitting, can't show that a dog is the smartest of millions of dogs.
fact: In the Second World War Britain spread the idea that carrots helped pilots see at night, partly to hide its new radar (a way of finding enemy planes in the dark with radio waves); carrots have vitamin A, which helps keep eyes healthy, but they don't give night vision.
fact: Bulls can't tell red from green well; they charge at the moving cape, not at its colour.
fact: Brain scans show activity all over the brain; over a day we use nearly all of it, not 10%.
fact: In careful studies where nobody knew which children had sugar, the sugar didn't change how they behaved; the excitement of a party is a likelier cause.
fact: Anyone can make a poster, so ask who made it and why; a poster from someone selling something might not tell the whole truth.
fact: Swallowed gum usually passes through your body in a few days, not seven years (but gum is still for chewing, not swallowing).
fact: Ostriches don't bury their heads in the sand; they lie low with their necks along the ground and turn their eggs in nests dug in the sand.
fact: Colds are caused by viruses caught from other people; being chilly with wet hair doesn't create a virus.
fact: Words people use: "my guy" means mate; "innit" (London) means "isn't it"; "got played" means got tricked; "sus" means suspicious; "missy" is what grown-ups call a cheeky girl; "rad" (US, older) means great; "bummer" (US, older) means "what a shame"; "dead" (Liverpool) means very.
exercise: Toby: "Sharks can't get cancer." Ellie: "How do you know?" Toby: "I just know." What is the problem? | "I just know" gives no reason or source | Toby is too young to know about sharks | Sharks are too big to be studied | Nothing; feeling really sure is a good enough reason
exercise: What have scientists found about sharks and cancer? | Sharks can get cancer; tumours were found in them | Sharks never get ill with anything | Eating shark cures cancer | Sharks only get cancer in zoos
exercise: Mia's cousin: "You can see the Great Wall of China from space with your eyes." "Who saw it?" "Everyone says it." What do astronauts say? | It is very hard or impossible to see with bare eyes | Every astronaut sees it clearly, even from the Moon itself | It is the only thing visible from space | It glows at night, so it's easy to spot
exercise: Why is the Great Wall hard to see from space? | It is very long but narrow | It is painted the colour of the sky | It is built underground | It is under cloud all year
exercise: Josh: "My dog is the smartest dog in the world." "How do you know?" "He can sit!" What is wrong? | One trick can't show he's the smartest of millions | Dogs that can sit are never smart | Josh hasn't measured how tall and fast his dog is yet | Only cats can learn to sit
exercise: Grandad: "Carrots help you see in the dark. In the war, the pilots ate carrots." What is the real story? | Britain spread the story partly to hide its radar | Pilots really could see in the dark from carrots | Carrots were the only food in the war | Radar was invented by a carrot farmer
exercise: What do carrots really do for your eyes? | Their vitamin A helps keep eyes healthy | They give you real night vision, like a cat | They slowly turn the colour of your eyes orange | They cure short sight, so you need no glasses
exercise: Ava: "Bulls hate red. That's why matadors use red capes." What really makes a bull charge? | The moving cape | The red colour | The matador's hat | The noise of the crowd
exercise: Leo: "We only use 10% of our brains." What do brain scans show? | Activity all over the brain; we use nearly all of it | Exactly 10% lights up all day long | 90% of the brain is empty space | Only geniuses use more than 10%
exercise: Priya's uncle: "Sugar makes kids hyper." Priya: "How do they know it's the sugar and not the party?" What did careful studies find? | Sugar didn't change behaviour when nobody knew who had it | Sugar makes every child twice as wild | Only fizzy sugar makes kids hyper | Parties have no effect on children
exercise: Max: "It's true, I saw it on a poster." What is a good question to ask? | Who made the poster, and why? | What colour is the poster printed in? | How big is the poster on the wall? | Where can I buy that poster myself?
exercise: Harper: "If you swallow gum, it stays in your stomach for seven years." What is true? | It usually passes through in a few days | It stays in your stomach for exactly seven years | It stays in your body for ever | It turns into a bubble inside you
exercise: Kai's mate: "Ostriches bury their heads in the sand when they're scared." What do they really do? | Lie low with their necks along the ground | Bury their heads in the sand so they can't see danger | Hide their heads under their wings | Dig tunnels to escape danger
exercise: Ellie's nan: "You'll catch a cold if you go out with wet hair." What causes colds? | Viruses caught from other people | Cold air blowing on wet hair outside | Getting caught in the rain without a coat | Eating ice cream in the middle of winter
exercise: What is the best first question when you hear a surprising claim? | "How do you know?" | "Who is the loudest?" | "Is it on a T-shirt?" | "How many likes has it got?"
test: Ellie: "You 'just know'? Then where'd you hear it, my guy?" Is she aiming at Toby or at his claim? | At his claim; she asks for the source | At Toby; she is saying he is stupid and young | At sharks; she thinks they're silly | At nobody; she agrees with him
test: Mia: "Everyone says it, but did any astronaut say it? That's the question, innit." Why is her question better than "everyone says"? | It asks for someone who actually looked | It asks how many people keep repeating the claim | It asks which country built the Wall | It asks whether the Wall is popular
test: Josh's friend: "Bro, my nan's dog can sit and give a paw. Is she the second smartest dog in the world?" What is the friend showing? | Sitting is too common to prove "smartest" | His nan's dog really is smarter, because she does two tricks | Giving a paw is the real test | All dogs are exactly as smart
test: Ruby: "Grandad, I looked it up. You got played by 1940." What tricked Grandad? | A wartime story spread to hide radar | A real study of pilots who ate carrots | A cartoon about a rabbit | Advice from a doctor today
test: What would happen if a matador waved a green cape at a bull? | It would charge at the moving cape all the same | It would ignore anything that isn't red, like green | It would run away from green | It would sit down calmly
test: The teacher: "Sorry, Leo, no hidden superpowers." What would someone need to back up the 10% claim? | A way to measure it, like brain scans, and they show the opposite | A famous film that says it | A poster with a brain on it | Nothing; everybody already knows it
test: Priya: "Bouncy castle and 20 screaming friends, but it's the sugar? Sus, Uncle." What does Priya suspect? | Another cause, the party, is being missed | Sugar is what makes the castles bounce so high | Her uncle secretly ate all the sugar himself | Her friends are made of sugar, like in the rhyme
test: Max's sister: "I made a poster saying I'm queen of the house. Doesn't make me queen, Max." What is her point? | Anyone can make a poster; ask who and why | Posters always tell the truth, because someone printed them | Only queens can make posters | Max is the king of the house
test: Harper's dad: "Did someone wait seven years and check? With a torch?" What is he really asking? | Where the "seven years" number came from | How long a piece of gum takes to chew properly | Who sells the best and brightest torches in town | Why chewing gum is so very sticky in your tummy
test: Kai: "If they buried their heads, they'd suffocate. There'd be no ostriches left. Think, bro." What is Kai doing? | Checking what would follow if the claim were true | Attacking the person who said it | Saying everyone believes it | Quoting a famous person
test: Nan in Liverpool: "Don't you get smart with me, missy, dry that hair or you'll catch your death." What is the truth? | Colds come from viruses; drying your hair is just nicer | Wet hair makes cold viruses grow | Nan is wrong, so never dry your hair | Wet hair always gives you a cold
test: Gen X dad: "Bummer, kid, your 'fact' needs a source. Totally rad that you checked, though." What is he praising? | Checking where a claim came from | Never believing anything at all, just to be safe | Believing whatever sounds cool | Repeating facts quickly
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of the response "I just know" when offered in support of an assertion. | It provides no reason or source for the assertion | It provides an excessive quantity of evidence for the assertion | It proves the assertion to be false | It is an instance of the fence-post error
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the visibility of the Great Wall of China from space to the unaided eye. | It is very difficult or impossible to discern | It is clearly visible from the surface of the Moon | It is the sole structure visible from space | It is visible only at night
exam: It is given that, during the Second World War, it was reported in Britain that carrots improved pilots' vision at night. Ascertain the principal purpose of this report. | To conceal the use of the newly developed radar | To increase the sale of carrots to the public during rationing | To report the results of a medical trial | To discourage the consumption of carrots
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of the common cold? | It is caused by viruses transmitted from other persons | It is caused by going outdoors with wet hair | It is caused by cold weather alone | It is caused by the consumption of ice cream
